\title{\bfseries Robust Global Virtual-Node Graph Neural Networks for Crystal Band-Gap Prediction with Cross-Source Label Consistency Analysis}
\author{Jiarui Liu}
\date{July 2026}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Crystal band-gap prediction is a central regression task in computational materials discovery, and two obstacles interact in it: standard message passing provides no explicit global information pathway, and band-gap labels from different databases are not equivalent, which can silently change model rankings. A structure-first audit between Materials Project (MP) records and a proprietary DFT band-gap database provided by Hongzhiwei Technology (HZW) shows that structural correspondence does not imply label agreement, since metal/non-metal conflicts and large same-class band-gap differences persist among matched structures. On the resulting 84,550 equal-atom structures, evaluated twice under MP and HZW labels with an identical split, we compare CGCNN, ALIGNN, and M3GNet baselines against a family of ALIGNN variants that differ in how global information is collected, broadcast, and regularized: plain and attentive virtual nodes, global attention, dual-slot tokens, physics-inspired constraints, top-\(k\) aggregation and broadcast-decay schedules, and a robust Huber objective. The design that transfers is the simplest one. A single plain virtual node improves ALIGNN on both label sources, and replacing mean squared error with a Huber loss improves it further; both gains are significant under a paired bootstrap and keep their sign in every run across three independent training seeds, giving test MAEs of \(0.2230\pm0.0016\) eV on MP labels and \(0.1936\pm0.0010\) eV on HZW labels. More elaborate global mechanisms do not survive the same test. Broadcast decay attains the lowest single-run MP MAE, 0.2237 eV, but that margin lies within test-set sampling noise, reverses sign on HZW labels, and does not replicate across seeds; cross-label evaluation attributes the divergence to training dynamics rather than to the evaluation labels. Size-stratified representation diagnostics supply a size-conditional mechanism, showing that the global token preserves late-layer node diversity in large structures. The robust, transferable design is therefore not the most complex architecture but ALIGNN with a single plain virtual node and a Huber objective.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} crystal graph neural networks; band-gap prediction; ALIGNN; virtual node; Huber loss; Materials Project; data consistency; global token.

\section{Introduction}

The electronic band gap is one of the most important properties for screening semiconductors, insulators, photovoltaic materials, optoelectronic materials, and catalytic candidates. First-principles calculations provide high-quality electronic-structure estimates, but they are too expensive for exhaustive exploration over large materials spaces. Machine learning, especially graph neural networks (GNNs), offers a practical route to predict material properties directly from crystal structures.

Crystal GNNs represent periodic materials as graphs whose nodes are atoms and whose edges encode local neighborhoods under periodic boundary conditions. CGCNN introduced a crystal graph convolutional architecture for material property prediction \cite{xie2018cgcnn}. ALIGNN further improves the geometric representation by combining an atom graph with a line graph so that bond-angle information can be explicitly modeled \cite{choudhary2021alignn}. M3GNet extends material graph learning toward universal interatomic potentials and many-body interactions \cite{chen2022m3gnet}. These models demonstrate that local geometry is highly informative for materials prediction.

However, local message passing has an inherent limitation: long-range or graph-level information must be transmitted through multiple layers. Increasing depth can introduce over-smoothing, in which node representations become progressively indistinguishable \cite{li2018deeper,oono2020graph,rusch2023survey}, and the finite receptive field of local message passing also creates an information bottleneck---over-squashing---in which long-range dependencies are compressed through a fixed-size representation \cite{alon2021bottleneck,topping2022understanding}. The two problems motivate both architectural remedies \cite{zhao2020pairnorm,rong2020dropedge,chen2020gcnii,xu2018jknet} and, more directly, the use of global tokens or virtual nodes that aggregate graph-level context and broadcast it back to local atom representations \cite{gilmer2017neural,battaglia2018relational,ying2021transformers,cai2023connection,southern2024understanding}; Section~\ref{sec:related} traces that lineage and its theoretical basis. The central modeling question of this project is therefore: for crystal band-gap prediction, does adding explicit global information improve a strong local geometric backbone, and if so, which global mechanism is actually stable?

The answer is complicated by a second issue: data labels are not always consistent across sources. Early experiments on the HZW band-gap dataset (a proprietary DFT database provided by Hongzhiwei Technology, in which each record cross-references a Materials Project entry)%
\footnote{HZW denotes the band-gap database of Hongzhiwei Technology (Shanghai) Co., Ltd., accessed through the provider's cloud API and used with permission; the underlying first-principles computational protocol is documented by the provider.
} suggested that single virtual nodes, dual global slots, and physics-inspired constraints could improve ALIGNN. When the same ideas were transferred to MP band-gap labels, however, the ranking changed and complex global mechanisms became unstable. This discrepancy forced a data-level audit rather than another round of blind architectural tuning.

We therefore pursue the modeling question and the data question together. On the data side, a structure-first MP--HZW audit separates structural correspondence from label agreement and shows that the two must be treated as distinct questions. On the model side, a controlled sequence of global-information variants sharing one ALIGNN backbone separates architectural changes from changes of the regression objective, with every close comparison supported by paired-bootstrap confidence intervals and by replication across three training seeds. Evaluating both label sources on the same 84,550 structures then answers the modeling question conservatively: the design that survives is ALIGNN with a single plain virtual node and a Huber objective, and size-stratified representation diagnostics give conditional evidence that the global token preserves node diversity where it should matter most, in large structures. Mechanisms that looked promising in isolation do not survive the same test. Global broadcast decay, for example, reduced systematic bias and RMSE on MP labels in the seed-42 run but not on HZW labels, and produced no seed-stable MAE gain on either source; we therefore report it as a source- and seed-sensitive refinement rather than as a recommended component.

\section{Background and Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

Crystal graph neural networks differ mainly in how much geometry they make explicit. CGCNN represents a crystal as a graph and learns local chemical environments through graph convolution \cite{xie2018cgcnn}; it is simple, interpretable, and effective as a baseline, but its geometric representation is mostly local and edge-centered. ALIGNN improves on this by introducing a line graph in which bonds become nodes, enabling explicit bond-angle message passing \cite{choudhary2021alignn}. This distinction matters for the present target, because local coordination geometry and bond angles are closely related to electronic structure, which is why ALIGNN is a strong baseline for band-gap prediction. M3GNet further incorporates many-body geometric information and was designed as a more general materials graph framework \cite{chen2022m3gnet}. Related geometric architectures include SchNet with continuous-filter convolutions \cite{schutt2018schnet}, the MEGNet graph-network framework \cite{chen2019graph}, an improved CGCNN variant that incorporates Voronoi-tessellated structural information \cite{park2020icgcnn}, directional message-passing schemes \cite{gasteiger2020dimenet,gasteiger2021gemnet}, and equivariant message-passing models \cite{schutt2021painn,batzner2022nequip,batatia2022mace}. Attention-based crystal models pursue the same long-range goal: periodic graph Transformers such as Matformer \cite{yan2022periodic} and ComFormer \cite{yan2024comformer}, and the molecular graph transformer of Anselmi et al.\ \cite{anselmi2024mgt} extend receptive fields through self-attention, while PotNet approximates complete interatomic potentials through efficient infinite-body summations \cite{lin2023potnet} and CHGNet provides a charge-informed universal neural-network potential \cite{deng2023chgnet}. We adopt ALIGNN as the backbone throughout, because it is consistently the strongest conventional baseline across the settings tested here.

What all of these architectures share is that graph-level information must travel through local edges. Virtual nodes are the standard remedy: a virtual node collects information from all graph nodes and redistributes the global representation back to each of them. The idea originates as the master node in message-passing neural networks for quantum chemistry \cite{gilmer2017neural}, is generalized to graph-level context vectors in the graph-network framework \cite{battaglia2018relational}, and reappears as the special \([\mathrm{VNode}]\) token in graph Transformers such as Graphormer \cite{ying2021transformers}. It also has a theoretical justification: message passing augmented with a virtual node can approximate the self-attention layer of a graph Transformer arbitrarily well \cite{cai2023connection}, so a virtual node is a computationally cheap substitute for full global attention; recent analyses further show that virtual nodes relieve over-squashing while their own benefits depend on node heterogeneity \cite{southern2024understanding}. Over-smoothing supplies a complementary motivation, since repeated local mixing drives node representations toward indistinguishability as depth grows \cite{li2018deeper,oono2020graph}, and a global token provides a shortcut route for graph-level information that does not require deeper local propagation; the depth problem has also been addressed architecturally by pair-wise feature normalization \cite{zhao2020pairnorm}, edge dropout \cite{rong2020dropedge}, initial-residual connections \cite{chen2020gcnii}, and jumping-knowledge aggregation \cite{xu2018jknet}. In materials science, virtual nodes have recently enabled full phonon-band prediction \cite{okabe2024virtual}. Cost is not the only argument for the simpler mechanism. Because the band gap is often dominated by local bonding chemistry, an overly powerful global attention module can inject noise or overfit source-specific artifacts, which is precisely the failure mode observed later in this paper. We therefore compare simple virtual nodes against more complex global-attention and dual-slot variants rather than assuming that more global capacity is better.

The second axis of this study concerns the labels rather than the model. Materials data may contain method-dependent labels, database-version differences, missing auxiliary quantities, or inconsistent metal/non-metal classification, and label noise of this kind is a recognized problem for deep models in general \cite{song2022learning} and for benchmark test sets in particular \cite{northcutt2021pervasive}. Band gaps are particularly method-sensitive: large-scale benchmarks show systematic differences across exchange--correlation functionals \cite{borlido2019large}, consistent with the known tendency of semilocal density functionals to underestimate band gaps \cite{perdew1985density,kuisma2010kohn}, the improved accuracy of screened hybrid functionals \cite{heyd2003hybrid}, and the recent assessment of meta-GGA functionals for solids \cite{kingsbury2022performance}, so two DFT databases can disagree even for the same relaxed structure. The disagreement is most severe near the zero-gap boundary, where regression and metal/non-metal classification meet. Robust regression is the natural response. Huber loss is a standard robust objective \cite{huber1964robust} that remains widely used in modern deep learning \cite{girshick2015fast,barron2019general,meyer2021alternative}: it behaves like mean squared error for small residuals and like mean absolute error for large ones, which suits a setting in which most labels are useful but a small fraction are noisy or heavy-tailed. Materials Project is a major public database for computational materials data \cite{jain2013mp}, alongside JARVIS \cite{choudhary2020jarvis}, the Open Quantum Materials Database \cite{kirklin2015oqmd}, and AFLOW \cite{curtarolo2012aflow}; Matbench provides standardized materials-property benchmarks \cite{dunn2020matbench}, and band-gap regression is itself an established machine-learning target \cite{zhuo2018predicting,ramakrishnan2015big}, addressed both through physics-based electronic descriptors that let shallow models learn crystal properties from small samples \cite{su2021physics} and through interpretable \(\Delta\)-machine-learning corrections that map semilocal PBE gaps onto hybrid-functional gaps \cite{zhang2022deltagap}. Those approaches correct a known offset between two levels of theory; the situation studied here is different, because the disagreement between two DFT databases is not a single systematic shift that can be learned away. The practical consequence pursued here is that even structurally matched MP and HZW records can carry non-equivalent band-gap labels.

\section{Data: Structure-First Audit and Dual-Label Construction}
\label{sec:data}

The prediction target is the crystal band gap in eV. Each input sample provides atomic species, fractional coordinates, lattice information, and a periodic-neighbor graph construction; ALIGNN-based models additionally use a line graph to represent bond-angle relationships. The primary metric is test MAE, with RMSE, \(\rdsq\), metal/non-metal F1, best epoch, training time, and peak GPU memory as auxiliary diagnostics.

Building a dual-label benchmark from two databases requires that structural correspondence and label consistency be established separately, and in that order. The HZW dataset, obtained from the cloud API of Hongzhiwei Technology (Shanghai) Co., Ltd.\ and used with permission, contains 100,000 band-gap records, all but 29 of which carry a Materials Project cross-reference id. The MP band-gap labels are Kohn--Sham gaps computed at the GGA-PBE level of density functional theory, with GGA\(+U\) corrections applied to certain transition-metal compounds \cite{jain2013mp}; band gaps at this level of theory systematically underestimate experimental values \cite{perdew1985density}, so all MP-label results should be read as predictions of the PBE gap rather than the experimental gap. We first paired HZW and MP records using crystal composition, atom count, lattice volume, fractional coordinates, and periodic structure matching (with pymatgen \cite{ong2013pymatgen}), and only after this step did we count label conflicts. The complete audit is summarized in Table~\ref{tab:data-audit} and Supplementary Figure~S1.

Two different threshold conventions are involved and must not be mixed. The \emph{audit statistics} (Table~\ref{tab:data-audit}, panel A) use diagnostic thresholds chosen to characterize disagreement: a record is counted as metal-like if its gap is \(\le 0.05\ \ev\) or it is flagged metallic, as non-metallic if its gap is \(\ge 0.10\ \ev\), and same-class numerical disagreement is counted above \(1\ \ev\). The \emph{dataset filter} (panel B) that actually constructs the T-series population uses a stricter zero-gap definition (\(\le 10^{-6}\ \ev\)) and removes only direct zero/nonzero conflicts and non-equal atom-count relations. Each panel's ledger closes on its own terms; entries from different panels are not comparable by subtraction. A third threshold pair defines the metal/non-metal F1 metric and is given in Section~\ref{sec:setup}.

