The final T-series starts from the 92,787 structure-matched records, removes zero/nonzero band-gap conflicts and non-equal atom-count relations, and retains same-class numerical disagreement and volume-per-atom differences. The resulting 84,550 structures provide a controlled cross-source comparison without silently treating the two label sources as identical; the filtering ledger is shown in Supplementary Figure~S1.

\section{Methods}

\subsection{Backbone, global token, and robust objective}

Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} gives the complete forward path of the model that this study ultimately recommends. A periodic crystal is converted into an atom graph and its line graph; coupled ALIGNN updates encode atom, bond, and angle states; a single virtual node exchanges graph-level context with the atom states; and the pooled atom representation and global token are read out jointly to predict the band gap, with the final robust model optimized under a Huber loss. The data audit of Section~\ref{sec:data} precedes training and is therefore not part of the forward architecture.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{\input{figures/paper/fig1_architecture.tex}}
\caption{End-to-end model architecture. (a) Periodic crystal input and atom/line-graph construction. (b) Coupled ALIGNN updates encode local atom, bond, and angle geometry. (c) A single virtual node exchanges graph-level context with the atom states by broadcast and pooling; the decay variant (T10 only), drawn as a dashed branch, changes only the epoch-dependent broadcast coefficient \(\gamma_t\), which is held at one in the recommended T4 and T7 models. (d) Joint local/global readout predicts the band gap under a Huber objective. The structure-first audit of Section~\ref{sec:data} precedes training and is shown as Supplementary Figure~S1.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

Three conventional models anchor the comparison. T1/E1 is CGCNN, which provides a local crystal graph convolution baseline; T2/E2 is ALIGNN, which augments the atom graph with a line graph and is the strongest conventional baseline in the controlled comparison; and T3/E3 is M3GNet, a material graph baseline with many-body geometric modeling. All enhanced variants are evaluated relative to these baselines, and especially relative to ALIGNN, since every enhancement described below is built on the ALIGNN backbone and changes exactly one thing at a time.

The first of these changes, and the only architectural one that survives every later test, is a single plain virtual node. Before layer \(l\), the current global token \(\mathbf{g}^{(l)}\) is broadcast to every atom by identity addition,
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathbf{h}}^{(l)}_i = \mathbf{h}^{(l)}_i + \mathbf{g}^{(l)},
\end{equation}
the broadcast atom states are then pooled back into the token by mean aggregation and a residual update,
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{g}^{(l+1)} = \mathbf{g}^{(l)}
 + \phi\!\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\tilde{\mathbf{h}}^{(l)}_i\right),
\end{equation}
and the backbone layer operates on the broadcast states:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)}_i =
\mathrm{ALIGNNLayer}(\tilde{\mathbf{h}}^{(l)},\mathcal{G})_i.
\end{equation}
The token is a learned parameter initialized to zero and shared across graphs, and the learned nonlinearity \(\phi\) is a two-layer perceptron that acts on the token update, so that the global-to-node broadcast path itself remains a plain identity addition. The pool--broadcast exchange is applied once before each of the four ALIGNN layers and each of the four edge-gated convolution layers (eight exchanges in total), and the readout concatenates the mean-pooled atom representation with the final token before the regression head. This model is denoted E4 or T4 depending on the dataset stage. Because it uses neither handcrafted size gates nor attention slots, it is a clean and interpretable global-information baseline, and it is the reference against which the more elaborate mechanisms of Section~\ref{sec:alt-global} are judged.

The second component of the recommended model changes the objective rather than the architecture. The robust loss used in T7 is the smooth-\(L_1\) (Huber-type) loss with transition point \(\beta=0.5\ \ev\), which is how it is implemented:
\begin{equation}
L_{\beta}(e)=
\begin{cases}
\frac{1}{2\beta}e^2, & |e|\le \beta,\\
|e|-\frac{1}{2}\beta, & |e|>\beta,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where \(e=\hat{y}-y\). This is the standard Huber loss rescaled by \(1/\beta\); it is quadratic for small residuals and linear for large ones, with a continuous derivative at \(|e|=\beta\). Applied to the band-gap regression output, it retains precise fitting for ordinary samples while reducing the gradient impact of large-error or inconsistent-label samples, which is exactly the situation the MP--HZW audit exposes: label disagreement survives filtering and produces heavy-tailed errors. The single transition value \(\beta=0.5\ \ev\) was fixed a priori from the residual scale of the baseline rather than tuned; a sensitivity check over \(\beta\in\{0.25,0.5,1.0\}\) (Supplementary Table~S15) confirms that the sign of the Huber gain over the MSE variant is invariant to \(\beta\) on both label sources; the spread across \(\beta\) (up to 0.006~eV on MP) exceeds T7's seed-level standard deviation (0.0016~eV), and the single-seed \(\beta=0.25\) probe is in fact slightly better on MP; we did not adopt it because selecting \(\beta\) on test results would constitute post-hoc tuning, so we claim only that the sign of the Huber gain is robust across \(\beta\).

\subsection{Alternative global mechanisms}
\label{sec:alt-global}

Three further families of mechanism were explored on the same backbone. They are reported here because their failure to transfer across label sources is part of the result rather than an omission from it.

The first family adds physical structure to the objective. A physics-inspired variant introduces auxiliary supervision from conduction-band minimum (CBM), valence-band maximum (VBM), band-gap consistency, and non-negativity:
\begin{equation}
L = L_{\mathrm{gap}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{aux}} L_{\mathrm{CBM/VBM}}
 + \lambda_{\mathrm{phys}} L_{\mathrm{gap=CBM-VBM}}
 + \lambda_{\mathrm{nonneg}} L_{\mathrm{nonneg}}.
\end{equation}
Because CBM/VBM values can be missing or invalid, an effective mask is used,
\begin{equation}
m_i = \mathbb{1}\left(|\mathrm{CBM}_i|>\epsilon \lor |\mathrm{VBM}_i|>\epsilon\right),
\end{equation}
so that auxiliary and consistency losses are computed only when \(m_i=1\). This direction helps in early HZW experiments but is not used as the final MP-label main path, because complete and reliable CBM/VBM labels are not always available.

The second family adds global capacity rather than physical constraints. Early HZW bucket analysis suggested that two attentive virtual nodes can help large structures even when they do not improve the overall average, which motivated size-supervised dual-slot variants: one stable global slot is retained, and a second slot is added whose usage is encouraged by a size-related supervision signal. The important difference from a direct handcrafted gate is that \(\texttt{num\_atoms}\) does not enter the forward gate; it only provides a training signal for whether the second global slot should be active. This idea works on early HZW labels but does not transfer reliably to MP labels, and the resulting interpretation is that atom count is not a universally stable proxy for when extra global capacity is useful: the value of additional global capacity depends on the label source, target distribution, and noise pattern.

The third family leaves capacity unchanged and instead selects or schedules the flow of global information. Top-\(k\) global aggregation computes node scores and uses only selected nodes in the global collection,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{K}=\mathrm{TopK}(s_i,k),\quad
\mathbf{g}=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{K}}\alpha_i\mathbf{h}_i,
\end{equation}
where \(k=\mathrm{clip}(\lceil0.2n\rceil,k_{\min},k_{\max})\), with \(k_{\min}=4\) and \(k_{\max}=32\); T8 isolates top-\(k\) under MSE, while T6 combines top-\(k\) with Huber loss. Warm-up top-\(k\) softens the transition by using mean aggregation in early epochs and moving gradually toward top-\(k\):
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{update}} =
(1-\alpha_t)\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{mean}}
+ \alpha_t\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{topk}}.
\end{equation}
Global broadcast decay acts on the opposite, global-to-node direction. Instead of always injecting the full virtual-node signal, T10 scales the broadcast by an epoch-dependent coefficient \(\gamma_t\),
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathbf{h}}^{(l)}_i =
\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_i + \gamma_t\,\mathbf{g}^{(l)},
\end{equation}
and leaves the rest of the exchange unchanged. The coefficient is held at 1 until epoch 10, decreases linearly to a floor of 0.25 by epoch 50, and is held there afterwards. The intention is to use strong global context in early training and to reduce global noise in late training.

All schedule constants---the top-\(k\) fraction \(0.2\), the clip range, the warm-up window, and the decay schedule---were chosen once from prior design experience and were not individually tuned, and every variant is trained under the same optimizer and budget so that comparisons reflect the mechanism rather than per-variant search. A decay-strength sensitivity check over the minimum scale \(\{0.5,0.75\}\) versus the default \(0.25\) (Supplementary Table~S15) shows that no decay floor makes T10 reliably better than the no-decay T7; on HZW labels the MAE in fact improves monotonically as the floor approaches the no-decay limit.

\subsection{Experimental setup}
\label{sec:setup}

All final T-series models use the relaxed clean equal-atoms dataset with 84,550 accepted records and a 67,640/8,455/8,455 train/validation/test split, and Table~\ref{tab:model-variants} lists the variants retained in the main comparison. Throughout the paper, models are indexed T1--T10; the ``T series'' denotes this final matched-benchmark stage, while the earlier exploratory stage (E series) and the strict-filter stage (S series) appear only in Section~\ref{sec:results-main} and the Supplementary Information (Supplementary Section~S10 gives the full nomenclature and Supplementary Table~S6 the descriptive-name mapping). The same structure split is evaluated twice: once with MP band-gap labels and once with HZW band-gap labels. Models are trained for up to 50 epochs, and test metrics are reported from the best validation epoch; per-epoch training and validation curves for all six core models on both label sources are given in Supplementary Figure~S3. The primary metric is MAE in eV. All ALIGNN-based variants use the AdamW optimizer \cite{kingma2015adam,loshchilov2019adamw} with a one-cycle learning-rate schedule \cite{smith2019superconvergence} and gradient clipping (mixed precision disabled); the full optimizer, learning-rate, batch-size, scheduler, and hardware settings are listed in Supplementary Table~S14. All models were trained on a single RTX~4090 (24~GB) under Slurm.

The metal/non-metal F1 metric carries its own threshold convention, the third in this paper. It treats ``metal'' as the positive class with an asymmetric threshold pair: a reference label is metallic when its band gap is \(\le 10^{-8}\ \ev\) (i.e.\ a database-reported zero gap), while a prediction is counted as metallic when it falls below a tolerance of \(0.1\ \ev\), because a continuous regressor almost never outputs exactly zero. F1 is the harmonic mean of the resulting precision and recall. Because zero/nonzero conflicts were already removed during dataset construction, the metal reference labels are identical for the MP and HZW versions of each structure; this is why T1 attains nearly identical F1 on both sources. A sensitivity analysis over prediction thresholds \(\{0.01,0.05,0.1\}\ \ev\) is reported in Supplementary Table~S11.

Because the MAE differences between the strongest variants are small, model pairs are compared with a paired bootstrap over the shared test set \cite{efron1979bootstrap,efron1993introduction}, following the recommended practice of confidence-interval-based comparison of learning algorithms \cite{demsar2006statistical}: for each pair we resample the test indices with replacement (\(B=10{,}000\), fixed seed), apply the same resampled indices to both models, and report the 95\% confidence interval of the MAE difference and the fraction of resamples in which one model wins (Supplementary Table~S7). This quantifies test-set sampling noise and is orthogonal to training randomness. The reproducibility utilities seed Python, NumPy, PyTorch \cite{paszke2019pytorch}, and DGL \cite{wang2019dgl}; the split is fixed at export time and does not depend on the training seed, so multi-seed runs vary only weight initialization and mini-batch order. Additional seeds use validation-MAE early stopping (patience of 8 epochs) to bound compute; the best-validation-checkpoint convention is identical across all runs, and across the 24 multi-seed runs the longest observed pre-best stall is 6 epochs, so this patience provably did not alter any multi-seed result (it triggered only for M3GNet, one epoch before the budget). The sensitivity and tuning-probe runs in the Supplementary Information report their own best/actual epoch counts. The six core models were each retrained with seeds 1042 and 2042 in addition to the original seed 42; we verified that the test-set sample identities are byte-identical across seeds, and Table~\ref{tab:main-results} reports mean \(\pm\) standard deviation over the three runs.

