T3 & M3GNet baseline & Material graph baseline with many-body geometric modeling. \\
T4 & ALIGNN + single plain virtual node & Stable global-token augmentation. \\
T7 & ALIGNN + single plain virtual node + Huber & Huber-only ablation relative to T4. \\
T10 & ALIGNN + virtual node + Huber + broadcast decay & Late-stage decay of global-to-node broadcast. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\section{Results}

\subsection{Cross-source comparison of global-information variants}
\label{sec:results-main}

The early exploratory stage on HZW labels (the E-series; see Supplementary Tables~S1, S5, and~S6 for definitions) motivated virtual nodes, dual slots, and physics-inspired objectives, and those exploratory results, including the size-supervised dual-slot variant with a validity-masked physics loss, are retained in Supplementary Table~S1 because they used an earlier data stage and are not directly comparable with the final controlled setting. What makes them worth reporting is that transfer to full MP labels reversed several of their rankings: attentive and size-supervised dual-slot variants were not reliably better than a single plain virtual node. The strict-filter stage (the S-series, trained under stricter label-consistency filters) nevertheless showed a size-dependent signal, with a dual plain node improving the \(>80\)-atom tail while losing on common medium-size structures; full bucket results are provided in Supplementary Table~S2. The controlled T-series below was designed to settle these reversals on a single fixed population.

Figure~\ref{fig:main-results} and Table~\ref{tab:main-results} report that comparison on exactly the same 84,550 structures. ALIGNN is the strongest conventional baseline. A single plain virtual node improves MAE on both label sources, and this gain is statistically significant under the paired bootstrap (T4 vs.\ T2: MP \(\Delta\mathrm{MAE}=-0.0075\), 95\% CI \([-0.0121,-0.0028]\); HZW \(-0.0104\), CI \([-0.0147,-0.0060]\)). Huber loss then provides the most stable additional improvement: T7 reaches 0.2249 eV on MP labels and 0.1940 eV on HZW labels (seed-42 values; Table~\ref{tab:main-results} reports three-seed means), and the T7-over-T4 gain is likewise significant on both sources (MP \(-0.0076\), CI \([-0.0122,-0.0029]\); HZW \(-0.0078\), CI \([-0.0120,-0.0035]\)). Broadcast decay gives the lowest single-run MP MAE, 0.2237 eV, but its advantage over T7 is not statistically resolved (MP \(\Delta\mathrm{MAE}=-0.0012\), CI \([-0.0058,+0.0033]\), spanning zero), and on HZW labels decay is worse than T7 (\(+0.0030\), CI \([-0.0011,+0.0071]\)). We therefore treat T7 and T10 as statistically indistinguishable in MP MAE and highlight T10 only for its reduced prediction bias and RMSE in the seed-42 run. Retraining with two additional seeds confirms this reading. Across three independent seeds, the virtual-node gain (T4 vs.\ T2) and the Huber gain (T7 vs.\ T4) keep their sign in every run on both label sources (direction consistency 3/3), with seed-averaged magnitudes of \(-0.0080\pm0.0006\) and \(-0.0088\pm0.0013\) eV on MP labels and \(-0.0104\pm0.0011\) and \(-0.0086\pm0.0010\) eV on HZW labels. The decay margin, in contrast, does not survive retraining: on MP labels the seed-averaged T10\(-\)T7 difference collapses to \(-0.0004\pm0.0017\) eV with a sign flip in one of three seeds, and on HZW labels it averages \(+0.0018\pm0.0023\) eV (worse than T7), likewise with a sign flip in one of three seeds. Figure~\ref{fig:main-results}(c) makes the factorization explicit: the global-token and Huber gains transfer across sources and survive both the paired bootstrap and seed replication, whereas decay is source-dependent and within noise on both axes. Figure~\ref{fig:statistical-evidence} summarizes the same statistical evidence directly: every virtual-node and Huber confidence interval excludes zero on both label sources, while both decay intervals cross it. Per-pair bootstrap intervals are listed in Supplementary Table~S7.

