One entry of Table~\ref{tab:main-results} warrants clarification before the analysis proceeds: the M3GNet baseline (T3) is weaker than ALIGNN on both label sources. M3GNet here is trained from scratch on the same data as the other models, not fine-tuned from a released potential, and uses the standard small configuration of the \texttt{matgl} implementation (three blocks, 128 hidden units, a 5.0~\AA{} cutoff) with no task-specific tuning; its full configuration is listed in Supplementary Table~S14. This is a deliberate same-budget comparison, but it does mean M3GNet is not presented under its most favorable setting. Two points make the observed ranking unsurprising. First, M3GNet was designed as a universal interatomic potential for energies, forces, and stresses \cite{chen2022m3gnet}, not primarily as a band-gap regressor, whereas ALIGNN's explicit bond-angle line graph directly targets the local geometry that controls electronic structure; ALIGNN is accordingly among the strongest published models on public band-gap benchmarks such as Matbench's \texttt{matbench\_mp\_gap}. Second, the observed gap does not appear to be a tuning artifact under single-factor probes: doubling the epoch budget to 100, halving the learning rate to \(5\times10^{-4}\), and doubling the hidden width to 256 units each leave the deficit essentially unchanged or make it worse (best probe 0.2867 vs.\ the \(0.2897\pm0.0033\) seed band of the baseline and 0.2400 for T2; single-seed probes, joint search out of scope; Supplementary Table~S16), and T3 reaches its best validation epoch well before its budget in every setting, so the gap is not under-training either. Because the central claims of this work are the T2\(\rightarrow\)T4\(\rightarrow\)T7 virtual-node and Huber ablations---all built on the same ALIGNN backbone---the absolute standing of M3GNet does not affect them; we retain it only as a representative many-body baseline.

\subsection{Chemical and symmetry structure of the error}
\label{sec:physics-analysis}

To connect the aggregate metrics to materials physics, Figure~\ref{fig:physics-analysis} resolves the test error of the robust model over the periodic table, the seven crystal systems, and the band-gap range. Three observations stand out. First, the error is chemically structured (the absolute per-element map is shown in Supplementary Figure~S2): hydrogen-bearing compounds show the highest per-element MAE (0.395~eV), and the light \(2p\) block (B--F) is systematically difficult (0.28--0.33~eV), consistent with the strong covalency and wide gap dispersion of these chemistries; the lowest errors occur for platinum-group \(4d/5d\) elements whose compounds are predominantly zero-gap metals. Second, the improvement from the virtual node and Huber loss is chemically broad rather than niche: relative to the ALIGNN baseline, the robust model lowers the per-element MAE for 58 of the 64 elements with at least 30 test structures (Figure~\ref{fig:physics-analysis}(a)), with the largest gains in \(5d\) and post-transition chemistry (Pt \(-0.040\)~eV, followed by La, Ir, Sb, Hg, I, and Bi) and only marginal degradations for the remaining six elements. Third, the error falls strongly with increasing crystal symmetry, from \(\approx0.30\)~eV in triclinic and monoclinic cells to 0.12--0.17~eV in hexagonal and cubic cells (the trend is not strictly monotonic---trigonal cells, for example, are harder than tetragonal ones---but the low-to-high-symmetry contrast is roughly a factor of two), and the robust model's gain over the baseline is largest precisely in the low-symmetry triclinic class (\(\approx-0.026\)~eV); low-symmetry cells are on average larger and chemically more diverse, which links this trend to the size-conditional global-token analysis of Section~\ref{sec:oversmoothing-discussion}.

The band-gap-resolved view in Figure~\ref{fig:physics-analysis}(c) also exposes an honest trade-off of the robust objective. Metallic (zero-gap) structures are the easiest regime (\(\approx0.10\)~eV), while near-metallic gaps in \((0,0.5]\)~eV are the hardest (\(\approx0.40\)~eV)---exactly the regime in which the MP--HZW audit found the largest labeling ambiguity. Huber loss improves every bucket at or above 2~eV on both label sources but concedes a small amount of accuracy in the hardest near-metallic bucket, consistent with a robust loss down-weighting the heavy-tailed, ambiguously labeled samples concentrated near the metal/non-metal boundary.

