\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig5_physics_analysis.pdf}
\caption{Physics-resolved error analysis of the robust model (T7, single virtual node + Huber). (a) Per-element MAE change from the ALIGNN baseline to T7 on MP labels over the periodic table; a structure containing element \(X\) contributes to \(X\), blue indicates improvement, white indicates no change, elements with fewer than 30 test structures are grayed, and elements absent from the test split are left white. The corresponding absolute per-element MAE map is retained as Supplementary Figure~S2. (b) Test MAE by crystal system (ordered by increasing symmetry) for the baseline, virtual-node, and robust models on MP labels; per-class counts are given below the axis. (c) Test MAE by true-band-gap bucket for the same models; solid lines with filled markers evaluate MP labels and dashed lines with open markers evaluate HZW labels.}
\label{fig:physics-analysis}
\end{figure}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Robustness, not global capacity, is what transfers}

The cleanest ablation is T4 versus T7. The architecture is the same single-virtual-node ALIGNN; only the final regression loss changes from MSE to the smooth-\(L_1\) Huber objective. On MP labels, MAE improves from 0.2325 to 0.2249; on HZW labels, from 0.2018 to 0.1940 (seed-42 values here and in Section~\ref{sec:decay-discussion}; three-seed means are given in Table~\ref{tab:main-results}). Both improvements are statistically significant under the paired bootstrap (both 95\% CIs exclude zero; Supplementary Table~S7). This is the strongest cross-source evidence in the project. It is consistent with the structure-first audit: even after removing zero/nonzero metal conflicts, the dataset still contains same-class numerical disagreement and heavy-tailed errors, so robust regression is useful. A complementary evaluation-side check supports the same reading: when the test set is partitioned by the strict label-consistency criteria (Supplementary Table~S10), all models are far more accurate on the label-consistent subset, and on MP labels both the virtual-node and Huber gains concentrate there (the virtual-node gain is \(-0.0081\ \ev\) on the consistent subset but essentially vanishes on the inconsistent complement), whereas on HZW labels the gains persist on both subsets. A strict \emph{training}-lens replication closes the loop: retraining the virtual-node model with the Huber loss directly on the strict-clean dataset (S-series run S14) improves the MAE from 0.2001 (its MSE counterpart, S-series run S4) to 0.1936. This single strict-lens run reproduces a gain of the same size as the relaxed-lens T4\(\rightarrow\)T7 step, which argues against---though, being a single run, cannot definitively exclude---the possibility that the Huber improvement merely absorbs label noise that only the relaxed filter admits (Supplementary Section~S15).

Figure~\ref{fig:mechanism-diagnostics}(a) summarizes the residual-quantile comparison. Huber reduces median and upper-quantile errors for both sources, supporting a robust-objective interpretation rather than a single-epoch artifact. The complete training and validation curves are given in Supplementary Figure~S3 and the residual histograms in Supplementary Figure~S4.

The converse of this result is equally informative: the mechanisms that add or select global capacity do not produce comparably stable gains. Top-\(k\) global aggregation provides no stable independent gain, since T8, the top-\(k\)-only model, is slightly better than T4 on MP labels but worse than T4 on HZW labels, while T6 and T9 both include Huber loss, so their gains cannot be attributed to top-\(k\) alone; we therefore treat top-\(k\) as an exploratory information-selection mechanism rather than a primary contribution, and we do not sweep its fraction parameter because it changes no conclusion. Raw capacity fares no better. Dual slots and attentive virtual nodes increase model capacity, but they also require the model to decide when extra global information is useful and what each slot should represent. Early HZW and strict-clean large-structure buckets show a real signal, in that large structures may benefit from more global capacity; medium-size structures, however, dominate the dataset and can be harmed by always-on additional global nodes. Without a reliable sample-level router or target-specific supervision, the extra capacity often learns source-specific noise rather than transferable structure-property information. Against this background, the value of the single plain virtual node is that it adds a global pathway without adding a decision the model has to get right.

\subsection{Broadcast decay is a property of the training source}
\label{sec:decay-discussion}

The key cross-source question is why global broadcast decay works on one label source but not the other. Decay is an information-flow schedule rather than a universally stronger architecture: early epochs use strong global-to-node broadcasting, while late epochs reduce the scale. On MP labels, this reduces global-token over-correction; T10 lowers MAE from 0.2249 to 0.2237 and RMSE from 0.5035 to 0.4973. On HZW labels, the same schedule removes useful late-stage calibration; MAE worsens from 0.1940 to 0.1970 and RMSE from 0.4458 to 0.4536. We emphasize that both MAE differences are within paired-bootstrap noise and that neither direction is stable across the three training seeds (Section~\ref{sec:setup}); the robust reading is not that T10 is better or worse in MAE, but that in the seed-42 runs it shifts bias and RMSE in opposite directions on the two sources. Figure~\ref{fig:mechanism-diagnostics}(b) summarizes this divergence, and the complete residual table is provided in Supplementary Table~S4.

Rather than leave the divergence at the level of interpretation, we provide three data-based diagnostics. First, a cross-label evaluation (Supplementary Table~S8) exploits the shared structure split: we score each trained model against \emph{both} label sets. The sign of the T10-minus-T7 gap follows the \emph{training} source and is invariant to the evaluation label---an MP-trained T10 beats MP-trained T7 even when both are scored on HZW labels, while an HZW-trained T10 is worse than HZW-trained T7 under either label. Decay's effect is therefore a training-dynamics phenomenon, not an evaluation-label artifact (seed-42 checkpoints; the four individual deltas each lie within the paired-bootstrap noise band, so the evidence is the consistent sign pattern rather than any single comparison). Second, the per-epoch validation trajectories (Supplementary Figure~S7) show that on HZW the T10-minus-T7 validation-MAE gap stays positive throughout the decay window and even grows late in training, whereas on MP it oscillates near zero; the harm tracks the shrinking broadcast scale. Third, a cohort analysis of the HZW structures most and least harmed by decay (Supplementary Table~S9) finds only weak chemical/structural separation: the decay-harmed cohort is modestly enriched in transition-metal-bearing compounds (\(+4.8\) percentage points, 95\% CI \([+0.1,+9.3]\)) and depleted in medium-symmetry and small-gap semiconductors, but no single family cleanly predicts the effect. We report this weak separation honestly rather than over-interpret it.

Taken together, the practical conclusion is conservative and now evidence-backed: T10 is a training-source-specific refinement whose benefit does not transfer, while T7 remains the robust cross-source model.

\subsection{Do global tokens address over-smoothing?}
\label{sec:oversmoothing-discussion}

A plausible mechanistic explanation is that the global token may reduce embedding homogenization in large graphs. In deep local message passing, atom embeddings can become increasingly similar because information is repeatedly mixed along graph edges. A virtual node provides a separate graph-level route: it stores global information in one token and broadcasts it back without requiring all context to pass through progressively deeper local propagation.

To test this mechanism, we performed a hook-based diagnostic on the saved T-series checkpoints. The diagnostic captured atom embeddings after each ALIGNN/edge-gated layer for T2, T4, T7, and T10 on the full held-out test set (8,455 structures per label source); a 240-sample stratified pilot with rotating atom-count-bucket quotas preceded the full run and agrees with it in the direction of every significant effect (the pilot protocol is described in the Supplementary Information). We measured three final-layer quantities: mean pairwise cosine similarity among atom embeddings, node-feature variance, and graph Dirichlet energy, the last following its use as an over-smoothing measure by Cai and Wang \cite{cai2020note}. Lower cosine, higher variance, and higher Dirichlet energy are interpreted as evidence against embedding collapse. Every reported delta relative to T2 is accompanied by a 95\% bootstrap confidence interval over the test structures (Supplementary Table~S12).

The all-sample result is mixed: global-token variants have higher node-feature variance and Dirichlet energy than T2 (with intervals excluding zero), but their mean pairwise cosine is also higher rather than lower. The data therefore do not support the strong claim that global tokens universally solve over-smoothing. The large-structure subset is coherent. For structures with more than 80 atoms (\(n=422\)), T4 and T7 both show the full relief signature---lower final-layer cosine, higher variance, and higher Dirichlet energy---on both label sources, with all confidence intervals excluding zero; T10 reproduces the cosine and variance effects on both sources, but its Dirichlet delta, while significant on MP, crosses zero on HZW, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:mechanism-diagnostics}(c,d) and Table~\ref{tab:oversmoothing}.

Thus, the defensible conclusion is size-conditional. We also note a metric caveat: of the three diagnostics, only the pairwise cosine similarity is invariant to the overall embedding scale, so the variance and Dirichlet-energy increases could partly reflect larger embedding norms rather than genuinely richer structure; we therefore treat the large-structure cosine reduction as the primary evidence and the other two metrics as corroboration. The single virtual node is not merely a score-improving trick; for large structures, it also preserves more late-layer node diversity than the ALIGNN baseline. This is evidence consistent with alleviating over-smoothing in the regime where global information is most likely to matter. It should not be phrased as a universal mechanistic proof.

