\subsection{Scope and limitations}

Several boundaries qualify these conclusions. The study is deliberately scoped to band-gap prediction, and band gaps are strongly influenced by local chemistry, bonding, and electronic-structure calculation settings, so no single-target study can fully demonstrate the value of global information; dielectric, piezoelectric, phonon, or transport-related targets may expose stronger global dependencies. We nevertheless keep the comparison single-property, because the paper's central axis is the dual-label cross-source design and the HZW database provides no auxiliary targets, so a second-property experiment would necessarily abandon that design; within this scope, the size-conditional representation diagnostics of Section~\ref{sec:oversmoothing-discussion} are the appropriate mechanism evidence. The final relaxed clean equal-atoms setting likewise keeps same-class large band-gap differences by design, which improves coverage but preserves label-source noise: Huber loss helps under this setting, but more detailed label provenance analysis would be needed for publication-grade claims.

The statistical basis is a second boundary. The headline metrics come from a fixed split; for the small differences among the strongest variants we report paired-bootstrap confidence intervals over the test set, and the six core models were retrained under three independent seeds, with both analyses agreeing that the virtual-node and Huber gains are robust while the T10-versus-T7 ordering is not statistically resolved. Three seeds remain a modest sample for estimating seed variance, and a larger seed count is left to future work; the split itself is held fixed by design so that MP and HZW evaluate identical structures. The hook-based over-smoothing diagnostic now covers the full held-out test set with bootstrap confidence intervals, but it analyzes the seed-42 checkpoints only, so repeating the layer-wise diagnostics across the three training seeds is the natural next step toward a definitive mechanistic version.

Finally, several models in this lineage were useful diagnostically but are not final models. Test-only routing, hard thresholds, and large-structure experts should not be presented as deployable methods unless their routing decisions are selected on validation data and evaluated once on held-out test data.

\section{Conclusion}

This work studies global virtual-node enhancements for crystal band-gap prediction and shows that data-source consistency is essential for interpreting model rankings. Within the controlled comparison, ALIGNN is the strongest conventional baseline, adding a single plain virtual node consistently improves it, and replacing MSE with Huber loss provides the most stable additional gain across both MP and HZW labels; both gains are statistically significant under a paired bootstrap and keep their sign in every run across three independent training seeds. Under the final relaxed clean equal-atoms setting, T10 attains the lowest single-run MP-label MAE of 0.2237 eV and T7 the lowest HZW-label MAE of 0.1940 eV, but the MP-label difference between T10 and T7 is within test-set sampling noise and does not replicate across seeds.

The final cross-source claim should therefore be conservative: ALIGNN + single plain virtual node + Huber loss is the most robust model design. Global broadcast decay reduced systematic prediction bias and RMSE on MP labels in the seed-42 run, but it does not improve MAE beyond paired-bootstrap noise on either source, its direction is not stable across training seeds, and the cross-label evaluation attributes what effect exists to training dynamics rather than to the evaluation labels; we therefore treat it as a source- and seed-sensitive refinement, not a recommended component. More complex attention, top-\(k\), and dual-slot mechanisms provide useful diagnostics but not stable final superiority. Nevertheless, the global-token results are not meaningless when overall MAE is unchanged: the single-token model improves ALIGNN under both label sources, and the dual-token analysis reveals a large-structure signal. Hook-based embedding diagnostics on the full held-out test set further show that, for structures with more than 80 atoms, both the single-token model and its Huber variant preserve lower final-layer pairwise cosine and higher node-feature diversity than the baseline, with confidence intervals excluding zero on both label sources. This supports a size-conditional over-smoothing interpretation, not a universal proof. Future work should extend the three-seed study to a larger seed pool, repeat the layer-wise diagnostics across seeds, and include full experimental ledgers in the Supplementary Information while keeping the main text focused on the core evidence.

\section*{Data and code availability}

The model code, training configurations, and analysis scripts that generate every table and figure will be released in a public repository upon publication. The MP-label subset (Materials Project identifiers, structures, and band-gap labels) is redistributable under the Materials Project terms and will be archived with a permanent DOI. The HZW band-gap labels are proprietary to Hongzhiwei Technology and were used with permission; they cannot be redistributed, but the list of Materials Project identifiers used, together with the audit and analysis code, is sufficient to reproduce the MP-label results and the structure-first audit. Access to the HZW labels is subject to the provider's terms. The intermediate result files underlying the main and supplementary tables---T-series summaries, data-quality audit tables, broadcast-decay analyses, and hook-based representation diagnostics---are retained with the project and provided in the Supplementary Information.

